# @clara/web — public config. NEXT_PUBLIC_* is exposed to the browser;
# NEVER put a secret in a NEXT_PUBLIC_* var, and never commit a real value
# here — copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and fill it there.

# Supabase Auth + PostgREST — cookie-session auth (lib/supabase/client.ts,
# lib/supabase/server.ts, proxy.ts) and the HUMAN governance lane later lanes
# build against (Accept-Profile/Content-Profile: clara). Invite-only: there is
# no self-serve signup, so these two values are the entire client-side surface
# — the anon/publishable key is publishable by design; the service_role key is
# NEVER used in this app (docs/plan/active/frontend-handoff-2026-08-23.md
# §0.4, §4.3 — "nothing connects to Postgres directly and no service-role key
# ever reaches the browser").
#
# Named ANON_KEY to match this repo's existing apps/dashboard/.env.example
# convention. Supabase's current dashboard/docs call the same value the
# "publishable key" (new key format, `sb_publishable_...`) — either key format
# works here; createBrowserClient/createServerClient just take a string.
#
# THE VALUE'S CLASS IS ENFORCED AT BUILD TIME. `pnpm --filter @clara/web build`
# runs `scripts/check-public-key.mjs` before bundling and REFUSES to build
# unless this slot holds an `sb_publishable_...` key or a legacy JWT whose
# decoded role is positively `anon`. A secret key, a `service_role` JWT, a
# malformed value or an empty slot all stop the build — `next build` inlines
# whatever is here into every browser bundle, and the variable's NAME proves
# nothing about its value (security review 2026-08-27, finding 7).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
